[h1]Lizarb Interface[/h1]

A complete reskin of RimWorld 1.6's interface. Window frames, buttons, tabs, tooltips, checkboxes, gizmos, scrollbars, fonts and the Architect menu.

[h2]Sixteen themes[/h2]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/settings/theme-grid.png[/img]

A theme is not just a palette. Each one changes the corner radius, the weight of the metal fillet, the corner ornament and the background pattern, so the shapes read as differently as the colours do. Iron is square and austere, Royal is wide and heavy, Aero is a translucent bubble with no outline at all, Gothic carries a trefoil in every corner.

Seven tileable background patterns, baked per theme so they carry that theme's own inks rather than a flat tint. Picking a theme applies its pattern, and you can swap it afterwards.

The first entry is [b]RimWorld default[/b], which hands every surface and the font back to the game.

[h2]Twenty-eight fonts[/h2]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/settings/font-picker.png[/img]

They ship inside the mod. Nothing to install, nothing to download, and every player gets the same list. Anything already installed on your machine can be picked as well, and the picker draws each name in its own face so you choose by looking rather than by guessing.

Three text sizes are adjustable, and every label can carry a real outline at a chosen thickness and opacity.

[h2]Every part of it is a setting[/h2]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/settings/tour.gif[/img]

Six tabs of them. Ten shapes for the Architect colour, seven opening animations, the gap between elements, the strength of the background texture, whether small text gets an outline, and a switch for each surface the mod touches.

[h2]The Architect menu[/h2]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/architect.png[/img]

Category buttons get a colour and an icon, so you find the one you want by sight instead of by reading. Categories added by other mods are handled too, without any patch, and the whole thing can be shaped or turned off in the settings.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]

This mod replaces the drawing of tabs, window backgrounds and menu sections rather than adding to them. Another mod that redraws those same surfaces will lose whichever of the two loads first, so load this one last if you want its look to win.

Buttons, window frames, tabs, widgets and scrollbars can each be handed back to the game on their own. A clash with one other mod costs one checkbox instead of the whole skin.

[h2]Source and licence[/h2]

[url=https://github.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface]github.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface[/url]

Code and textures are GPL-3.0, and the full source ships inside the mod folder. The fonts are under the SIL Open Font License, with each licence file included and every designer credited.

